WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.622
Before doing serious calculations with an array, inspect its structure.

00:00:04.622 --> 00:00:10.952
Four attributes are especially useful: dtype, shape, ndim, and size.

00:00:10.952 --> 00:00:13.652
Dtype tells us how NumPy is storing the values.

00:00:13.652 --> 00:00:16.502
In this example, we explicitly request floating-point values.

00:00:16.502 --> 00:00:20.327
Shape tells us the length of each dimension.

00:00:20.327 --> 00:00:26.322
The sales array has two rows and three columns, so the shape is two by three.

00:00:26.322 --> 00:00:28.752
Ndim tells us the number of dimensions.

00:00:28.752 --> 00:00:34.317
Because we have rows and columns, this is a two-dimensional array.

00:00:34.317 --> 00:00:36.692
Size tells us the total number of elements.

00:00:36.692 --> 00:00:40.007
Two times three gives us six values.

00:00:40.007 --> 00:00:42.792
Click Run and confirm each attribute.

00:00:42.792 --> 00:00:45.622
This habit becomes extremely important later.

00:00:45.622 --> 00:00:52.907
Many bugs in numerical code are not really mathematical problems; they are shape problems.

00:00:52.907 --> 00:00:59.042
If a calculation gives an unexpected result, checking shape and dtype is often one of the fastest ways

00:00:59.042 --> 00:01:02.347
to understand what went wrong.
